# KevBox Admin server environment — TEMPLATE.
# Copy to /etc/kevbox-admin/env on persovps and fill REAL values. This file is the
# ONLY place secrets live; it is never committed. (deploy.sh does not ship it.)
#
#   sudo install -m 600 -o kevbox-admin -g kevbox-admin /dev/stdin /etc/kevbox-admin/env < deploy/env.example
#   sudo -e /etc/kevbox-admin/env    # then edit in the real values
#
# Local port nginx reverse-proxies to (loopback only; must match nginx-admin.kevbox.dev.conf).
PORT=8787

# Direct Postgres connection for data access (server-side only, NEVER in the browser).
# TWO things matter here:
#   1) ROLE: use the LEAST-PRIVILEGED role `kevbox_admin` (manage member_addon + read
#      member emails only), NOT the `postgres` superuser. A compromise of this public-facing
#      process must not hand over the whole project. See deploy/README.md "Least-privileged DB role".
#   2) HOST: use the Supabase **Session pooler** connection string (port 5432). It is
#      IPv4-reachable and keeps prepared statements working (session mode). The direct host
#      `db.<ref>.supabase.co` is IPv6-ONLY and will fail to connect from an IPv4-only VPS;
#      the transaction pooler (port 6543) breaks pg's prepared statements — avoid both here.
#      Dashboard: Project Settings → Database → Connection string → "Session pooler".
# Keep ?sslmode=require. Copy the real host/user from the dashboard; shape:
SUPABASE_DB_URL=postgres://kevbox_admin.CHANGE_ME_projectref:CHANGE_ME_password@aws-0-CHANGE_ME_region.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres?sslmode=require

# Used to verify the admin's Supabase JWT (auth.getUser). The anon key is public-safe.
SUPABASE_URL=https://CHANGE_ME.supabase.co
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=CHANGE_ME_anon_key

# Comma-separated allowlist of admin emails (authenticated users NOT in this list are rejected).
ADMIN_EMAILS=kevin.chiha@gmail.com
